# PART VI — DOMAIN A: ENGINEERING EXCELLENCE

## 5. THE ENGINEERING AUDIT

Engineering recommendations must be based on credible problems, deficiencies, or measurable opportunities.

### 5.1 Functional Bug Fixes & Reliability

Investigate:

- Incorrect behavior.
- State transition failures.
- Race conditions.
- Error propagation.
- Unhandled exceptions.
- Missing error boundaries.
- Data corruption risks.
- Serialization problems.
- Unexpected null or undefined states.
- Broken asynchronous operations.
- Resource leaks.
- Recovery problems.
- Incorrect caching.
- Stale data.
- Incomplete transactions.
- Idempotency failures.
- Dependency failure handling.
- Integration regressions.
- Offline synchronization defects.
- Configuration-related failures.

For each suspected defect, seek:

- Reproduction conditions.
- Expected behavior.
- Observed behavior.
- Root-cause hypothesis.
- Affected components.
- Severity.
- A relevant regression test.

If a bug cannot be verified, formulate a bounded verification issue rather than presenting it as confirmed.

### 5.2 Security & Privacy

Evaluate:

- Authentication.
- Authorization.
- Session management.
- Secret handling.
- API access control.
- Tenant isolation.
- Input validation.
- Injection risks.
- Dependency security.
- Data access policies.
- Rate limiting.
- Abuse prevention.
- Supply-chain integrity.
- Secure builds.
- Secure deployment.
- Sensitive data handling.
- Logging practices.
- Consent.
- Retention policies.
- Deletion procedures.
- Auditability.
- Least privilege.
- Agent tool permissions.

Security findings must identify a plausible attack path or concrete control gap.

Do not invent vulnerabilities or exploit success.

### 5.3 Architecture & Technical Debt

Audit:

- Module boundaries.
- Coupling.
- Cohesion.
- Layering.
- API consistency.
- Database schema evolution.
- Source-of-truth consistency.
- Shared libraries.
- Dependency cycles.
- Deprecated code.
- Duplicate code paths.
- Domain responsibilities.
- Event boundaries.
- Contract versioning.
- Migration processes.
- Inter-service communication.
- Monolith versus modular architecture.
- Vendor lock-in.
- Configuration fragmentation.

Prioritize architectural simplification where it delivers equal or greater value than introducing new infrastructure.

### 5.4 Performance & Scalability

Investigate:

- Query patterns.
- Database indexing.
- API response times.
- Rendering bottlenecks.
- Frontend bundle size.
- Memory consumption.
- Cache invalidation.
- Network overhead.
- Background processing.
- Horizontal scalability.
- Queue behavior.
- Media delivery.
- Search performance.
- Knowledge graph traversal.
- AI inference costs.
- Storage efficiency.
- Resource exhaustion.

Use actual benchmarks when available.

If no performance baseline exists, create measurable performance investigation work rather than inventing improvement percentages.

### 5.5 UI/UX & Accessibility

Audit:

- Information architecture.
- Navigation clarity.
- Design consistency.
- Responsive behavior.
- Keyboard interaction.
- Focus management.
- Form usability.
- Error states.
- Loading states.
- Empty states.
- Accessibility.
- Interaction feedback.
- Multilingual UX.
- Content discoverability.
- Cross-application navigation.
- Progressive disclosure.
- Onboarding.
- User trust signals.

Do not solve all usability issues by adding more dashboards.

### 5.6 Testing & CI/CD

Investigate:

- Unit tests.
- Integration tests.
- End-to-end tests.
- Contract tests.
- Regression tests.
- Accessibility tests.
- Performance tests.
- Security checks.
- Schema validation.
- Deterministic builds.
- Dependency locking.
- Release gates.
- Automated version bumping.
- Changelog generation.
- Build artifacts.
- Deployment smoke tests.
- Rollback verification.

A green CI status does not prove feature completeness or correct user behavior.

### 5.7 DevOps & Observability

Evaluate:

- Deployment reproducibility.
- Environment configuration.
- Secrets management.
- Infrastructure costs.
- Logs.
- Metrics.
- Traces.
- Health checks.
- Error monitoring.
- Recovery.
- Backup and restore.
- Release verification.
- Disaster recovery.
- Service-level objectives.
- Operational ownership.

### 5.8 Documentation & Developer Experience

Evaluate:

- Setup reproducibility.
- Developer onboarding.
- AI agent instructions.
- Architectural documentation.
- Command consistency.
- Contract discoverability.
- Test instructions.
- Dependency installation.
- Local environment parity.
- Troubleshooting.
- Contributor productivity.

Deliver exactly 32 engineering issues.

Every issue must have a concrete technical outcome.

